                                  GRAPHICS.LIB

              A set of basic graphics routines for Microsoft C 4.0

                              by J. R. Applegate
                  Colorado School of Mines - Computing Center

GRAPHICS.LIB provides some basic tools for Graphics under MicroSoft C version
4.0 as well as MS Fortran and Pascal 3.31.  Both CGA and Tandy 1000 modes are
implemented, providing functions for mode and palette control, simple graphic
elements such as points, and complex graphic elements such as lines and
circles.  Tandy 1000 modes allow the use of 16 color medium resolution and
4 color high resolution graphics.  Additional routines provide for joystick
input and 3 voice sound on the Tandy 1000.  This revision incorporates
routines for the MicroSoft Mouse or Tandy DigiMouse.

These routines may be used with MS Fortran or Pascal by calling them as
C functions and using the large model library GRAPHICL.LIB.

All programs which use GRAPHICS.LIB should include GRAPHICS.H as this file
makes all the necessary function declarations as well as defining some useful
constants.  For the mouse functions a second include file MOUSE.H defines
constants and external variables which are used to pass back the mouse parms
to your program.  In order to access these variables they must be declared
as external in your calling program.

                             GRAPHICS.H Constants

/* Screen Modes */
#define BW40         0
#define CO40         1
#define BW80         2
#define CO80         3
#define MEDIUM_4     4   /* 320 X 200 - 4 colors */
#define MEDIUM_2     5   /* 320 X 200 - 2 colors */
#define HIGH_2       6   /* 640 X 200 - 2 colors */

/* Tandy 1000 - PC Jr. Modes */
#define LOW_16       8   /* 160 X 200 - 16 colors */
#define MEDIUM_16    9   /* 320 X 200 - 16 colors */
#define HIGH_4       10  /* 640 X 200 - 4 colors */

/* Colors */
#define BLACK         0
#define BLUE          1
#define GREEN         2
#define CYAN          3
#define RED           4
#define MAGENTA       5
#define YELLOW        6
#define LIGHT_GREY    7
#define DARK_GREY     8
#define LIGHT_BLUE    9
#define LIGHT_GREEN   10
#define LIGHT_CYAN    11
#define LIGHT_RED     12
#define LIGHT_MAGENTA 13
#define LIGHT_YELLOW  14
#define WHITE         15

/* normal aspect for circles */
#define ASPECT        270

/* JOYSTICK Definitions */
#define LEFT_X        0
#define LEFT_Y        1
#define RIGHT_X       2
#define RIGHT_Y       3
/* BUTTONS */
#define LEFT_RED      4
#define LEFT_BLACK    5
#define RIGHT_RED     6
#define RIGHT_BLACK   7

                          MOUSE.H Definitions
The following variables are added by MOUSE.H as external variables.  They must
be declared as external in your program before you can access them.

union REGS m_inregs;   /*holds interrupt input parameters*/
union REGS m_outregs;  /*holds interrupt output parameters*/
int mse_xin;           /*x-input coordinate*/
int mse_yin;           /*y-input coordinate*/
int prscnt[2];         /*array for count of button presses*/
int x_lstprs[2];       /*array of x-coords at last press*/
int y_lstprs[2];       /*array of y-coords at last press*/
int rlscnt[2];         /*array for count of button releases*/
int x_lstrls[2];       /*array of x-coords at last release*/
int y_lstrls[2];       /*array of y-coords at last release*/
int h_micks;           /*horizontal motion counter*/
int v_micks;           /*vertical motion counters*/

#define M_LEFT 0    /* Left button  */
#define M_RIGHT 1   /* Right button */

                            GRAPHICS.LIB Functions

void mode(m);
int m;

    Changes current screen mode to that specified in m.
    On a Tandy 1000 do not exit from the program while in modes 8-10 or the
    system will HANG.  Issue a mode(CO80); mode(BW80); before termination of
    of the program.

unsigned char getmode();

    Returns the current screen mode.

unsigned char wherex();

    Returns the x location of the current text cursor.

unsigned char wherey();

    Returns the y location of the current text cursor.

void gotoxy(x, y);
int x, y;

    Moves current text cursor to x, y.

void cls();

    Clears screen (including graphics).

unsigned char ppoint(x, y);
int x, y;

    Returns color of pixel at x, y.

void palette(t, p);
int t, p;

    If t (type) is 0 sets background to color p.
    If t (type) is 1 sets palette number to p.

void point(x, y, clr);
int x, y, clr;

    Sets pixel at x, y to clr (color).

void line(x1, y1, x2, y2, clr);
int x1, y1, x2, y2, clr;

    Draws line from x1, y1 to x2, y2 in color clr.

void box(x1, y1, x2 ,y2, clr);
int x1, y1, x2, y2, clr;

    Draws an empty box from x1, y1 to x2, y2 of color clr.

void circle(x, y, r, clr, aspect);
int x, y, r, clr, aspect;

    Draws a circle with a center at x, y of radius r amd of color clr.
    Aspect controls whether it draws a circle of elipse.  The constant
    ASPECT should yeild a perfect circle.

void sound(voice, frequency, level)
int voice, frequency, level;

    Generates a sound of certain frequency and a specified level (1-16)
    on 1 of 3 voices (1-3).  Sound should be stopped by issueing a call
    to each voice with a level of 0.

int stick(axes_num)
int axes_num;

    Returns the joystick value based on axes_num which is the same number
    as in basic and is defined as:
              Left X = 0
              Left Y = 1
              Right X = 2
              Right Y = 3

int button(button_num)
int button_num;

    Returns TRUE (1) if appropriate button is not pushed.  Note that this
    is different from expected (but I like it)!  Button numbers are:
              Left Red Button = 4
              Left Black Button = 5
              Right Red Button = 6
              Right Black Button = 7

int m_reset()

    Reset mouse driver and return current status
    Returns 0 if mouse not installed
    Returns -1 if mouse installed

void m_showcur()

    Increment cursor flag and display cursor
    Required after m_reset() to display cursor

void m_hidecur()

    Decrement cursor flag and hide cursor
    Will turn cursor off

int m_readloc()

    Read cursor position and status
    x location is returned in mse_xin
    y location is returned in mse_yin
    function returns button status:
            0 = no button
            1 = left button
            2 = right button
            3 = both buttons

void m_setloc(x, y)
int x, y;

    Set cursor position to x, y screen position

int m_keyprs(button)
int button;

    Get mouse button press information
    Increments prscnt if button is pressed
    sets x_lstprs and y_lstprs to previously pressed position
    function returns button status:
            0 = no button
            1 = left button
            2 = right button
            3 = both buttons

int m_keyrls(button)
int button;

    Get mouse button release information
    Increments rlscnt if button is released
    sets x_lstrls and y_lstrls to previously released position
    function returns button status:
            0 = no button
            1 = left button
            2 = right button
            3 = both buttons

void m_setxlim(xmin, xmax)
int xmin, xmax;

   Set horizontal minimum and maximum

void m_setylim(ymin, ymax)
int ymin, ymax;

   Set vertical minimum and maximum

void m_defgcur(hot_x, hot_y, curshape)
int hot_x, hot_y;
int *curshape;

  Define graphics cursor
  hot_x and hot_y are the cursor matrix hot spot
  curshape is an integer array that defines the cursor matrix
  see Mouse documentation for more information

void m_deftcur(cur_type, scr_msk, cur_msk)
int cur_type, scr_mask, cur_mask;

  Define text cursor
  cur_type is 0 - software cursor or 1 - hardware cursor
  see mouse doc for more info

void m_mickeys()

  Read mouse motion counters
  sets h_micks and v_micks

void m_inpmsk(call_msk, offset)
int call_msk, offset;

  Set user-defined subroutine call mask
  sets mouse software to branch to a specific offset on conditions
  set by call_msk... see doc for more info

void m_penon()

  Turn light-pen emulation on
  Does not work with Tandy's crappy Digi-Mouse

void m_penoff()

  Turn light-pen emulation off
  Does not work with Tandy's crappy Digi-Mouse

void m_setasp(h_ratio, v_ratio)
int h_ratio, v_ratio;

  Set horizontal and vertical motion sensitivity
  h_ratio = horizontal mickey:pixel ratio
  v_ratio = vertical mickey:pixel ratio

                                   DEMOS

CGADEMO.C and CGADEMO.EXE provide a demo for an IBM CGA.  T1KDEMO.C and
T1KDEMO.EXE use the 16 color graphics of the Tandy 1000.
SDEMO.C and JOYDEMO.C and their .EXE files have been added to demo the new
sound and joystick functions.
MOUSEDEM.C and MOUSEDEM.EXE work on CGA or Tandy to give a VERY simple
drawing program.
